	// LaTeX.sublime-settings -- settings for the LaTeX syntax, i.e. .tex
	// files. NOT .sty/.cls (those use the TeX syntax) and NOT .bib (Bibtex
	// syntax): neither sees anything set here. See "Scope" at the end of
	// this header.
	//
	// Maintained as part of the TeXLib bundle. Ships to coworkers via
	// TeXLib-Installer; sourced from texlib/Sublime/LaTeX.sublime-settings
	// in the TeXLib repo.
	//
	// --- added_words REPLACES the global list; it does not stack ---------
	//
	// Sublime resolves each key from the last file in the cascade that
	// defines it, and for an array that means REPLACEMENT, not union. The
	// added_words below therefore shadow any added_words in your global
	// Preferences.sublime-settings completely.
	//
	// Consequence: the "Add Word" command -- which writes to global
	// Preferences -- HAS NO EFFECT while you are editing LaTeX. The word
	// applies in every other syntax and silently does nothing in .tex.
	//
	// Measured on Sublime build 4200:
	//     Packages/User/Preferences.sublime-settings ...    8 added_words
	//     Packages/User/LaTeX.sublime-settings .........  637 added_words
	//     what a .tex view actually resolved ...........  637  (not 640)
	// The three words present only in Preferences were silently inactive in
	// every .tex file.
	//
	// --- where personal words go instead ---------------------------------
	//
	//   * Shared math vocabulary (terminology, mathematician surnames,
	//     journal names): add it to the array below in the REPO copy, then
	//     deploy. Do not hand-edit the deployed Packages/User/ copy --
	//     deploy.ps1 and TeXLib-Installer overwrite it, your edits with it.
	//
	//   * Personal vocabulary (collaborator names, lab or course-internal
	//     jargon): use a separate dictionary package -- not added_words,
	//     anywhere. Sublime 4's "dictionary" setting takes a LIST of .dic
	//     paths and consults every entry, so a second dictionary is
	//     genuinely additive (en_US stays in force), and it can be wired to
	//     .bib and .sty/.cls too, which added_words cannot. Such a package
	//     survives TeXLib re-deploys and never ships to coworkers.
	//
	//     !! Do NOT add a "dictionary" key to THIS file. Packages/User/
	//     !! wins outright for any key it defines, so a "dictionary" here
	//     !! would silently disable that package's list. If TeXLib ever
	//     !! genuinely needs one, it must list both .dic files itself.
	//
	// --- editing guidance ------------------------------------------------
	//
	//   * Sections are alphabetized -- keep them that way for diff sanity.
	//   * Keep the list to vocabulary a coworker would want too; it ships to
	//     everyone. Personal proper nouns belong in a dictionary (above).
	//   * Stuck-suffix artifacts ("ness", "th", "ech", "lder") have been
	//     intentionally excluded -- they mask real typos. If Sublime flags
	//     "Cech" or "Holder", add the accented form ("Čech", "Hölder")
	//     instead of the bare suffix.
	//
	// --- scope: .bib and .sty/.cls see none of this -----------------------
	//
	// added_words is per-syntax, and this file is the LaTeX syntax only.
	// TeXLib deliberately ships no Bibtex.sublime-settings /
	// TeX.sublime-settings: that would mean three copies of one array
	// drifting apart, for two file types a dictionary serves better anyway.
	// Sublime/README.md carries the full reasoning.

	// Keep the autocomplete popup free of snippet entries. LaTeXTools bundles
	// ~200 tab-trigger snippets (bf, frame, bca, Rar_upr, ...) that otherwise
	// flood the popup; TeXLib serves its own macros/environments via
	// texlib/texlib_complete.py instead. This ONLY hides snippets from the
	// popup list -- every snippet still expands on its tab-trigger. Pairs with
	// the completion suppressors in LaTeXTools.sublime-settings.
	"auto_complete_include_snippets": false,

